    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "By applying a cinematographic framework to T. S. Eliot’s Four Quartets, this paper reconsiders the relationship between the poet and the medium of film. Eliot’s own correspondence and lectures serve as irrefutable evidence of his ambivalent, if not, hostile attitude towards the cinema, forming a well-trodden path of scholarship. However, closer inspection reveals the significance of experimental film to Eliot in both theory and practice, influencing his theory of the objective correlative as well as Four Quartets. In addition to primary source materials, the paper benefits from the writings of those who knew Eliot, philosophical works (including Henri Bergson and the poet’s own dissertation), literary criticism, and film theory (specifically Jean Epstein and David Trotter). The paper also draws from Eliot’s letters to Emily Hale, unsealed in January 2020, and recent biographical texts based upon their correspondence.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
